  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nStrong, possibly severe thunderstorms will accompany a cold\nfront as it crosses the region Saturday afternoon and evening.\nLatest model guidance has bumped up the timing of frontal \npassage, now beginning to impact eastern Ohio counties as early \nas 18Z. NBM mean sfc CAPE tops out between 600 and 800 J/kg \nahead of the front where strong south/southwesterly flow pumps \ndewpoints to near or slightly above 60F, however, ensemble \nguidance still shows a wide spread with Tds and CAPE. Overall, \nover the past 24hrs of runs, instability has trended down \nslightly. Latest NBM guidance gives a probability of >1000 J/kg\nbetween 20-30% ahead of the front, and 60-70% for >500 J/kg. \nThe HREF mean is slightly lower with a similar spread. \nEnsemble joint probability of CAPE >50 J/kg and bulk\nshear >30 ranges between 30 and 50% along and ahead of the\nfront. In any case, the low CAPE high shear environment still \nposes a severe threat with damaging winds the primary impact. \nHail and an isolated tornado cannot be ruled out as well. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe well above-normal temperatures continue into Saturday as the\nridge axis shifts east of the region and strong warm advection \nramps up in southwesterly flow ahead of the front. Latest NBM \nguidance shows probabilities ranging from 60-80% of reaching \n>80F from Pittsburgh and south, decreasing to a 20-30% \nprobability along I-80.\n\nBehind the front, the script flips, and northeast CONUS \ntroughing leads to daytime high temperatures 10-15 degrees \nbelow normal for Sunday and Monday, before approaching \nclimatology on Tuesday. Frost/freeze products may need to be \nissued by early next week, and some Sunday night snowflakes are \na possibility. The probability of <32F lows is 30% in PGH \nMonday, increasing to near 80% along I-80, and 60-100% across \nthe region Tuesday morning.",
